WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.439
Film Courage: Why are there so many screenwriters
opposed to structure?

00:00:03.439 --> 00:00:07.310
Are they hurting or helping themselves with
this viewpoint?

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:16.380
Eric Edson: There are a lot of people (always
will be) some of them Sir Tom Stoppard himself

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:25.250
(no less a writer than Tom) is really opposed
to education, this education that is now offered

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.980
in graduate schools, screenwriting and all
that.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.369
But he can afford to be.

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:34.199
You know, if you’re born a genius…okay,
go for it!

00:00:34.199 --> 00:00:37.710
You can say anything you want, you can do
anything, and you write brilliantly and for

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:38.870
them, it’s natural.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:45.999
But for most of us poor unfortunates below
the peak of Olympus.

00:00:45.999 --> 00:00:49.280
Yeah, we have to work a little harder at it.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:57.829
I think…I think it is a misconception of
what there is to be learned.

00:00:57.829 --> 00:01:05.899
Many, many people have always done it a certain
way and they sit and suffer and think there

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:09.170
is a muse that comes down.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:16.951
Some people may be very intuitive about this
story stuff and for them…yeah…fine it

00:01:16.951 --> 00:01:17.951
can work.

00:01:17.951 --> 00:01:24.350
But what I find is for the vast majority of
people who want to be writers (screenplays

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.789
or novels), that’s not the case.

00:01:26.789 --> 00:01:29.619
There are tools, tools, tools.

00:01:29.619 --> 00:01:36.890
Tools one needs to master.

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:48.290
Before you are going to play inspirational
and fantastical orchestral music for instance.

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:50.010
You’re going to compose.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:54.810
Don’t you need to know how to play the piano?

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:55.890
Well, yeah…that helps.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:02:01.409
Don’t you need to know about music and the
theories of music and composition.

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:02.759
It would help.

00:02:02.759 --> 00:02:05.920
You’d save yourself a lot of time and trouble
if you’d do that.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.170
And I believe very much that it’s the same
with screenwriting.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:11.810
There is so much to learn.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:14.140
There really is.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:19.050
And the folks that tend to think that way
and defend it.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.760
Okay, that’s the way they’ve always worked.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:21.760
I get that.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.650
That’s the way you want to work.

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:24.650
Great.

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:25.650
Do it.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:31.470
But I believe they are bringing their own
lives far more emotional suffering than there

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:32.850
needs to be.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:35.910
It’s not the muse, folks.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:37.690
There is not muse.

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:42.220
It’s up to you to come up with a decent
idea so you can then pull out the correct

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:52.550
tools to start shaping into something that
will work and will bring emotional sustenance

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:53.910
and release for an audience.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:57.830
Film Courage: I think you had a blog post
where I just skimmed the beginning of it where

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:03.900
you wrote you know…We wake up, the birds
are chirping and we’re inspired.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.200
And we are going to write.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:10.380
And then we get into page 47 and we’ve hit
a roadblock.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:15.680
So is this what we’re talking about here,
sort of how to find a way out of that road

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:16.680
block.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:21.080
Because if we’re at page 47, which is this
point of the story.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:23.760
Are we at the mid-point by the way?

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.090
Eric Edson: Ahhhh…48ish.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:27.090
47ish.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:29.830
That’s about the mid-point.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:31.480
Yes.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:34.690
I have always said no plan.

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:38.410
No detailed plan, no Oscar.

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:42.850
It’s just not going to happen.

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:47.510
If you’re going to build a house (and especially
an intricate one, as one as intricate as a

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:54.480
screenplay for a really good movie, you have
to have architectural renderings that take

00:03:54.480 --> 00:04:00.160
it down to the tiniest dimensions and details
and piece of equipment that is going to be

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:01.920
used.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.790
You have to be organized about it.

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:09.520
Disorganized people (as a rule) don’t do
all that well as writers, screenwriters.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:12.450
Again, this is not everybody!

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:18.590
There are in fact geniuses (don’t you just
hate them?) out there who can do this stuff

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:21.660
just instinctively without even thinking about
it.

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:27.660
But there is a handful of them living on planet
earth at any given time.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.500
And of course maybe they can’t see it from
our perspective that ‘Gee maybe you’d

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.030
have to work at it?’

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:34.650
But, yes.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:38.340
There is a lot of work and a lot to be known.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:47.260
And isn’t it better if you lay the pieces
out…you have a puzzle board before you and

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:52.290
you have all the dark pieces here and the
light pieces here and you begin to search

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:59.430
and see and fit together something that is
going to be a full picture by the time you’re

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:00.690
through.

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:08.110
That is a systematic way of doing something
that is very difficult to do.

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:16.610
I would argue tools, tools, quantifying change,
understanding the 14 character categories.

00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:26.220
Knowing that there are 10 modes of plot coming
out of how many heroes and what the heroes

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.860
relationship is other characters.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:30.830
There’s only 10 of those.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:33.800
Once you have an idea, pick your 10.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:38.840
That’s going to save you a lot of time and
a lot of agony.

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:41.480
And I would also throw this out.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:46.580
I would challenge…listen I am open as anybody
to learn here.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:50.030
I am a life-long learner myself.

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:58.220
If someone thinks I am wrong here or has an
instance and if they can prove that I am wrong,

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:00.540
I’d be very interested to hear it.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:06.590
I really would If you think you’ve found
a 15th category (for instance)…if you’ve

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:12.419
taken a look at the 14 and understand what
is contained in the 14.

00:06:12.419 --> 00:06:16.450
If you can come up with a brand new character
category, I would love to be one of the first

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:21.900
to hear about it (really).

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.370
I offer that to the students all of the time.

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:28.510
Usually when I hear back, when they bring
things up, rather enthusiastically (bless

00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:36.510
their hearts), it’s usually from a misunderstanding
of the 14 or it’s a misunderstanding of

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:41.270
what I’m saying relative to hero goal sequences
and things like that.

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:43.940
But it’s okay.

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:50.230
I’m happy about hearing of ways in which
I might be incorrect with this stuff.

00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:51.110
There is room for growth.

